Date: Wed, 26 May 93 11:20:48 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: ECON/SPACE: Sell the Universe Whole

David Friedman offers an informative and thoughtful summary of a book
he read on U.S. homesteading.  Indeed, it seems difficult to auction
off land already in use by squaters, and I'd be reluctant to auction
off space if there were people living up there.  The window of
opportunity for an auction is only so long.  He finishes:

 There was, as I remember, one transaction more like what Robin proposes. A
 big chunk of land--I think in eastern Ohio, but it might have been western
 pennsylvania or New York--was sold to one buyer, who later subdivided and
 resold. My impression was that it worked a little better than the standard
 method, but not enough better, at least from the standpoint of those making
 the decisions, to become a model for future transactions.

To put a more positive spin on this history, I offer the following
quotes (reposted from last October) from:

 "Homesteading and Property Rights; or, `How the West was Really Won'",
 by Douglas Allen, J. Law & Econ, April 1991, pp. 1-23.

 Allen says "On the surface, the U.S. experience is puzzling because the
 federal government began disposing public land through auctions and other
 price mechanisms before it established the practice of homesteading."
 But he argues that U.S. realized that "On the western frontier, the
 alternative was not to use public lands as a source of revenue but,
 rather, to forego those lands to Mexicans, Indians, possibly Texans, or
 even the British, Spanish, or Russians."

 Already in an external race with other nations, "the U.S. government
 used settlers to lower the cost of enforcing state ownership ... By
 instigating homesteading, the U.S. government restricted the choices of
 settlers by providing an incentive to rush one area [at a time].  The
 sudden arrival of tens of thousands of people into a given territory
 destroyed much of the Indian way of life and forced the Indian tribes to
 accept reservation life or to join the union."

 In contrast, "Australia faced no threat from external powers and no
 serious internal threat from natives for crown land. ... Australia has
 never had a homestead policy and since 1825 has either sold or leased
 its public lands."

If the United Nations agrees to an auction, no external threats are
likely, and so rapid colonization is not needed, and the argument for
homesteading much weaker.

These examples make it hard for me to accept Tim Starr's claim that 
"There aren't any alternatives [to homesteading]".  Alternatives have
happened, and could happen again.  The question is: which do we want?

Dean writes:

 The more important argument is that 20 years (or whenever) down the
 line, enough people who had sold there shares early (been "swindled"
 out of their shares) would object to the system, and so the system
 would be junked, and the property rights would go out the window.
 MarkM's scheme lets us divide it up soon, but not start the trading
 till there was enough potential value there that people could see that
 there actually is value.

In principle, this makes sense.  Though share values would decline if
people expected the rights to not be enforced.  This should place a
natural limit on how concentrated the assets become. 

But I worry about the transaction costs of sending everyone share
certificates and policing trading restrictions.  And more
fundamentally, I worry that political negotiations are not well-suited
to fine tuning parameters like when people should "vest" in shares.
And if folks aren't fully vested after the auction, someone else must
hold those assets in trust, and there's a serious agency problem.

Similarly, while Hal is right that if we waited we might know better
how to divvy things up, even though if we wait too long, squatters
may prevent any auction.  But I'm not sure who I trust to make this
judgement.  

Instead I prefer simplicity.  Auction the universe whole to a single
buyer, and divide the proceeds immediately among the nations in
proportion to their population.  Any more complexity should be in the
form of limits on what that buyer can do with it.  That buyer could be
required to auction everything again in chunks each less than 5% of
total market value, and to do so before any commercial use of anything
that buyer owns.  

Robin Hanson

