Date: Wed, 26 May 93 13:37:25 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: ECON/SPACE: Sell the Universe Whole

Dani Eder suggests:

 A set of individuals establish a lunar development company.  Each
 contributes capital to the company, and gets some region of the Moon
 as 'his' in return.  The company and the individuals contract to
 recognize and protect each other's claims to the respective portions
 of the Moon.  Non-participants in the company are ignored.

If you think it plausible that governments will abandon their current
claims on the moon, and allow private organizations to negotiate or
fight to divvy it up, this is more plausible.  If governments won't
give up thier claims without being paid off somehow, then a
privatization auction does exactly that - pays them off.

Mike Price asks "Where do you get the figure $10-100 bn?"  I guessed.
I doubt the U.S. would pass up a chance to own the universe for less
than one year's NASA budget.  Hence the lower bound.  And I just can't
imagine more than $100 billion of capital tied up for decades in the
hope of eventual return when space is finally commercialized.  

Robin 



