Date: Wed, 26 May 93 15:46:07 PDT
From: ghsvax!hal@uunet.UU.NET (Hal Finney)
Subject: ECON: Property as Schelling point

I was puzzled by Robin's suggestion that the things we consider to be
private property could in a sense be a Schelling point.

Schelling points, as I understand, are places which have a certain
obviousness or attractiveness to them so that people can converge on
them without needing to communicate.  For example, if I want to meet
my friend in Hawaii, we would obviously go to the exact geometrical
center of the largest volcanic crater in the state and wait for each
other.

I can see some relations of this idea to property.  I am likely to try
to claim that something is property only if I imagine that others will
be willing to accept my claim.  So there is this sense of putting
yourself into other people's shoes and trying to imagine what their
ideas about property would be.

But it does seem like there are some important differences between the
situations involved in establishing notions about property and the
situations where I have heard Schelling points being talked about.
The biggest difference is that we can communicate our ideas about
property.  We don't have to just sit back and choose something so
obvious that other people have probably made the same choice.

People argue about property all the time.  One neighbor plants a tree
which will grow to shade another neighbor's solar water heater.  Who
has the right to the sunlight?  They will try to reach an agreement,
and if they can't they may go to court to get it decided.  Once it is
established who has the right, the other person can buy the right from
him if he can offer enough.

I think that debate, discussion, argument and court battles are how
we decide what is property and what is not, and I would expect this to
be true in the future as well.  We need to consider how other people
will react to our suggestions, of course, as we do in all parts of
life, but I don't see that the notion of Schelling points is that
relevant in the process.

Hal Finney
hal@alumni.caltech.edu

