Date: Thu, 27 May 1993 01:55:24 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: ECON/SPACE: Sell the Universe Whole

A couple corrections for this thread.  First, I don't know of any nation 
that claims sovereignty to any part of the moon, and even if they did it 
would violate international treaties which declare national sovereignty 
as ending at the top of the atmosphere.  Second, space *is* commercialized; 
the commercial communcations satellite business is on the order of $1 to 
$10 billion per year, depending on how you count it.  If Clarke orbit 
was private property instead of run by an international bureaucracy, 
the real estate there, orbital slots and frequencies, would probably 
be worth around $20 billion, far more than any other part of space
could obtain at auction.

Until Hughes Aircraft got into the game in the early 
1960's, Arthur C. Clarke was about the only person who recognized the 
importance of 24-hour orbit.  One socialist does not a market
make. :-)  The most valuable piece of space
real estate today wasn't even recognized as a scarce resource
until folks started using it.  This $multi-billion industry,
operating without any people on-site at the property in question,
also points out the distinction between right-by-usage and 
right-by-settlement.   Furthermore, any enforcement of Clarke Orbit 
claims would be done on Earth, not by physically controlling the 
property itself.  Right-by-usage seems to be the best way
to recognize claims to Clarke Orbit, and I claim that it is the
best way to establish property rights in general.

As Dan Davis points out, what constitutes "use" can be vague, as in the 
case of casual stargazers in the dark sky property rights issue, or 
those who wish to keep wild lands wild for preservation.  In the case of 
Clarke Orbit it what constitutes use is reasonably clear.  I take a 
reasonably practical approach; users are defined as:

* Those with a measurable commitment to operations which depend on
the claimed property
* Those with a strong motivation, and the military or political power 
to overrrule the arbitrator's decision.

For example, the amount of money invested by a person or group
in ground based astronomy equipment constitutes a measuable
commitment to dependency on the dark sky, and so the arbitrator
could distribute fungible shares of the dark sky proportionately
to equipment purchase receipts. The space-billboard operator would 
accept this decision if purchasing rights would be cheaper than 
fighting an unpopular political battle.  However, casual stargazers, 
billboard haters, militant environmentalists, etc. would be 
"disenfranschised" by  this decision, and if the had the motivation 
and power could nullify it.

Given the potential of modern computers to operate and track
markets and market-player reputations, I see a niche for companies 
that specialize in nothing else but defining and implementing new kinds 
of property rights.  Richard Sandor has his own company now (name escapes 
me at the momeent, sorry) creating new kinds of futures, especially
catastrophe and reinsurance futures which could revolutionize
risk distribution in the insurance market.  Another sub-niche
would be consulting with courts or private arbitrators to help
resolve disputes flexibly.  Stealing from Coase a simple
exmaple: "Railroad operator must underwrite or purchase $1 million 
policy to cover wheat farmer's fire damage.  Operator can negotiate for 
a reduction in premium with the insurance company in exchange for 
spark-arrestor devices, if mutually agreeable."  Another example could 
be consulting with the arbitrators in the astronomers vs. space billboard 
folks to create dark sky property rights.

Nick Szabo				szabo@techbook.com

