Date: Thu, 27 May 93 09:52:49 CDT
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: ECON/SPACE: The Walrasian Universe Auction Contd

Mr. Davis may have mentioned a significant point.  Definition of
property rights  is not important if the resource is not scarce.
Currently, the Moon is quite large in relation to the area that
has been used for anything at all, so there is no problem in
taking samples or even mining a million tons per year.  But in
the case of land on Earth, there are many places where population
realtive to land area has made land scarce.  I hypothesize that
it is precisely those places in which land ownership is the
most strongly held concept.

As negative examples, aborginal peoples like the Indians, Australians,
and Africans did not have strong ideas about land ownership.
People from cities always seem to have strong definitions of land
ownership.

Projecting into the future, when it is determined that there are
scarce resources on the Moon (such as permanently shadowed regions
at the poles with condensed volatiles, or permanently sunny regions
on polar mountains), and those scarce resources are being occupied,
that is when I predict a property claims system will be initiated.
Prior to that it is simply irrelevant.  After that, the lack will
lead to conflict.  Thus that is the optimum time to institute a
system.

Dani Eder

