SECTION: EXECUTIVE NOTEBOOK; Pg. 62

LENGTH: 1240 words

HEADLINE: RIGHT TO KNOW VS. RIGHT TO SAY NO -- Industry debates ethics
of government encryption standard

BYLINE: John P. McPartlin

BODY:

One month after President Clinton signed a National Security Directive
that orders a review of a leading computer privacy and encryption
technique, computer professionals continue to debate just where the
government's need to access information ends and where a user's right
to privacy begins.

The government's plan, backed by the FBI, the National Institute of
Standards and Technology, and the National Security Agency, stems from
intelligence agency concerns that their ability to eavesdrop on
encrypted computer communications, telephone lines, and fax
transmissions during their investigations be protected. The plan
dovetails with the FBI's ongoing efforts to pass legislation mandating
technology that would enhance its ability to wiretap digital telecom
lines (IW, Nov. 2, 1992, p.  13).

Under the new encryption plan, manufacturers would place electronic
"locks" or encrypters, called " clipper chips, " within computer or
communications devices.  Then, for example, if the FBI wanted to place
a wiretap, it could unlock those encrypters with an "electronic key."

The FBI says that before it can break the code, it would have to
obtain a warrant. In addition, the keys would always be held in escrow
by two organizations; once the FBI had obtained a warrant, those
organizations would then hand over the code-breakers to law
enforcement agents. The key holders could be government entities, such
as the Federal Reserve, or even a group within the FBI.

The plan has several groups worried. Two weeks ago, the Digital
Privacy and Security Working Group sent Clinton a letter expressing
reservations about the encryption proposal and requesting a meeting
with him. The group's members include both professional groups, such
as the Computer Professionals for Social Responsibility (CPSR), the
Information Technology Association of America, and the American Civil
Liberties Union, and several large vendors, including IBM, DEC, AT&T,
and Apple.

Markey's Mark

Rep. Edward J. Markey (D-Mass.) plans to begin hearings before the
House subcommittee on telecommunications and finance, which he chairs,
in the next few weeks to assess the plan. Markey, in a speech to the
National Computer Ethics Conference last month, said Americans should
have "the right to protect their privacy through any form of
encryption available to them."

Critics fear the security devices will include a secret "back door"
that would allow the FBI to place wiretaps without first obtaining a
warrant.  They also worry that the existence of the electronic keys
creates opportunities for computer pirates to break the codes and
access corporate information.

Although the NSA-developed encryption scheme is said to be classified,
the dangers of someone using reverse engineering to crack the system
are significant, according to the Electronic Frontier Foundation, a
Washington-based group led by Lotus co-founder Mitch Kapor.

Opponents further complain that the encryption system-developed in
secrecy by the NSA-lacks industry involvement and represents a secret
standard imposed on business by the government. They also warn that
the NSA's involvement signals an intrusion by the agency into
commercial technology standards.

"We worry about the precedent this sets in relation to the proposed
national information infrastructure and data highway projects," says
David Sobel, legal counsel for the Washington-bBI and the NSA are
increasingly asserting themselves in these areas and will claim they
have the necessary expertise."

Opponents' worst fear is that the encryption standard may have already
gained too much momentum. Last month, AT&T-despite its signature on
the Digital Privacy and Security letter to the White House-became the
first vendor to publicly announce its support of the standard. AT&T
also began providing the government with test versions of products
using the chip.

IS groups, however, are not among the concerned. The Society for
Information Management, for one, has "no official policy on the
encryption plan," says a spokesman. "It has not been addressed or even
talked about as far as I know."

But the CPSR and others say there's still time for IS professionals to
get involved.  "The industry needs to start paying attention," says
Sobel.  "Although it might seem esoteric right now, this will become a
very serious issue-a government standard with substantial market
pressure."

