naturally from the meaning of the two words it is made from, and enables
me to conveniently refer to the goal that Turing had in mind when he
proposed his test. You want the term to refer to an arbitrary collection
of things which will inevitably mislead people who are unfamiliar with
the historical accidents behind that classification. Your attempt to
deny that AI has failed (so far) is misleading because you are arguing
against something quite different from what David Friedman claimed.
The claim that expert systems are AI has mislead people into overestimating
the capabilities. I have yet to see a use of the broad definition of AI
that serves a constructive purpose.

>In any case, we don't yet know enough to aim at human-equivalent self-aware
>machines yet.  So we hack at pieces of the problem.  For better or for
>worse, that hacking is what AI is.

 We can aim at a goal without having practical ways of achieving that goal.
Most of the hacking that is currently classified as AI research has goals
which are more practical and ought to be labelled more honestly.
