> At Bellcore, we were thinking of emulating a nematode's neural network
> thoroughly on the Y Machine, a parallel supercomputer we'd built that
> was designed for doing large parallel simulations. We concluded we
> didn't have the resources, both in terms of manpower but also largely
> in terms of compute power to do the job right. The organism in
> question had only a couple hundred neurons.

So you claim sufficient understanding on the basis of a project never 
completed?

Signals in a neuron are frequency modulated.  Assume a signal bandwidth of
500 bits per second (cold blooded animals usually have lower bandwidths, 100
bits per second is probably more realistic).  Assuming every neuron in the
nematodes brain is connected to every other (which it is not) we have a total
traffic between neurons of substantially less than ten megabits per second. 
Diffuse chemical interactions are more complex, but they also have much lower
bandwidth.

If neurons are doing something simple, as you implicitly claimed, my desktop
computer can simulate a nematode and its environment, with no problem, by
using abstracted neurons.

If neurons are doing something complex, as seems more likely IMNHO, then we
are not at all sure what that something is.

I am unfamiliar with your project, and I am not familiar with nematode
brains, except that they are small, have fixed wiring, and that some
nematodes (I do not know how large their brains are) can determine whether
they are in a small enclosed environment or free to roam, and act differently
according to that circumstance.

I do know that the outcome of your project ("we could have done it with just
a bit more resources") is remarkably similar to the outcome of most attempts
to emulate simple brains, and similar to the early work in AI.  "The computer
would have been self aware, but we exceeded our budget."

What happens in most such projects is that the attempt to simulate fails
miserably.  To justify the failure one solution is to adapt the simulation
to lower and lower levels of abstraction of a neuron, until one run out of
power.  Doing a low level simulation of a hypothetical neuron is not valid
unless you have reason to believe that the low level abstraction is
qualitatively different, rather than subtly different, to the high level
abstraction.

If neurons were doing something simple, we would understand what it was that
they were doing, and high level abstractions of a single neuron would work. 
They do not, and their failure implies that low level abstractions will not
work either.

I also notice that "successful" emulations produce results remarkably similar
to those of "artificial intelligence" - rigid, inflexible, and fragile
behavior.

I think that emulations are great - provided someone else does them - because
their failure can be more educational than the failure of AI.

There is something we do not know - something important we are missing, and
emulations will help us focus on what that something is.  Blaming the
computer will not help us focus on what that something is.

