From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Is AI evidence for the existence of the soul?

This is partially responding to Peter McCluskey, who wrote:

>  I am trying to define the term AI in a way that is coherent, follows
> naturally from the meaning of the two words it is made from, and enables
> me to conveniently refer to the goal that Turing had in mind when he
> proposed his test.

How about a definition from AI people?  I'll pick one at random, the
first sentence of the Introduction to _The Handbook of AI, vol 1_:

  AI is the part of computer science concerned with designing
  intelligent computer systems, that is, systems that exhibit
  the characteristics we associate with intelligence in human
  behavior -- understanding language, learning, reasoning, solving
  problems, and so on.

That seems reasonable enough to me, modulo preconceived notions
about "computer systems" and "understanding".

As to AI failing so far, it has indeed -- in the same exact way
as fusion power generation, interstellar space exploration,
nanotechnology, economics (where are the accurate models?),
and robotics.  Like AI, all of these fields have produced
theoretical and practical results that can be applauded or
scoffed at, depending on the world-view of the audience.  Also
like AI, none of them seem particularly close to their "ultimate
goal".

Despite being an AI researcher myself, I find myself in agreement
with the thrust of David Friedman's argument, though I believe
the significance of the result to be rather weak.  How are we
to treat the predictions (informal though they were) by early AI
researchers for unreasonably fast progress?  It's easy now to
shake our heads and say they were silly -- but those were (and
are, mostly) smart people.  Apparently, they misunderstood the
difficulty of the problem.  More recently, the same kind of
doomed predictionism was exercised by Lenat re: the CYC project.
If these smart experts keep guessing wrong about what's required
to produce "real" AI, there seems some justification to reducing
the faith in today's predictions, which mostly have retreated to
direct neural modelling, by experts.

However:
  1) Note that only the extravagant (extravagantly wrong) predictions
  are remembered.  Most researchers are more cautious and wince when
  prominent people issue predictions to the press.

  2) The simple failure to achieve "real" AI (whatever that is) is
  not evidence against its possibility; the in-progress status of
  any research program in progress doesn't diminish its chance of
  success.  Nonetheless, the mechanistic view of mind is just a theory,
  and with a lack of hard evidence either for or against, it is accepted
  or rejected largely on faith.

There are really good things about the neuron-modelling paradigm:
Mainly, that it is so empirical.  If I were to start over again
in my AI studies, I might very well take that track -- but (as
said Darth Vader) it's too late for me.

Besides, the stuff I do is fascinating to me; it makes me wiggle
a bit to call it AI... I prefer to think of it as studying
large-scale heterogenous parallel information processing systems.
However, because the work fits in well with the definition I
gave earlier -- which is similar to that used by most AI people
I know -- "AI" is a fair enough characterization.

